WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:10.510
Science serves as a powerful tool for unlocking the mysteries of the universe, but understanding its limitations is essential for its effective application.
科学是揭开宇宙奥秘的有力工具，但要有效运用科学，了解其局限至关重要。

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:18.850
There are occasions where I have used the handle of a knife as a hammer, but the result would have been better if I’d had a more suitable tool at hand.
我曾有几次把刀柄当锤子用，但如果当时手边有一件更合适的工具，效果本会更好。

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:25.640
As far as science goes, it is really good at testing things that are testable, but not so for those that are not.
就科学而言，它非常擅长检验可检验的事物，但对不可检验的事物就不是这样了。

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.490
We can do, and have done, an impressive amount with our brains.
凭借大脑，我们能够做成、也已经做成了数量惊人的事情。

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:32.380
But there are limits.
但局限是存在的。

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:40.610
Sometimes these limits go away if we keep at it for long enough — we just need better facilities and experiments to get the answer.
有时候，只要我们坚持足够长的时间，这些局限就会消失——我们只是需要更好的设施和实验来得到答案。

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:44.810
Breaking new ground in modern science this way can be costly.
以这种方式在现代科学中开辟新天地，代价可能很高。

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:55.880
Next-generation supercomputers or incredibly large telescopes are expensive, yet these may be required to find answers to some of the unsolved mysteries of the universe.
下一代超级计算机或超大型望远镜造价高昂，但要为宇宙中一些未解之谜找到答案，可能就需要它们。

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:02.130
Sometimes the limits we encounter in trying to unlock the nature of the universe are cognitive.
有时，我们在试图揭开宇宙本质的过程中遇到的局限是认知上的。

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:08.802
Think about this: human DNA is only about 1.2 percent different from that of chimps.
想想这一点：人类的DNA与黑猩猩的DNA只相差约1.2%。

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:11.832
Chimps are smart, no question.
黑猩猩很聪明，这一点毫无疑问。

00:01:12.632 --> 00:01:14.912
But could you teach them advanced mathematics?
但你能教它们高等数学吗？

00:01:15.712 --> 00:01:20.062
What if our DNA were another 1.2 percent further evolved than it is?
要是我们的DNA比现在再多进化1.2%，会怎么样？

00:01:20.862 --> 00:01:22.832
What might our brains be capable of then?
到那时，我们的大脑可能会有怎样的能力呢？

00:01:23.632 --> 00:01:26.542
The level of abstract thinking might be unimaginable.
抽象思维的水平可能会超乎想象。

00:01:27.342 --> 00:01:29.892
Sometimes the limits we hit are fundamental.
有时，我们碰到的局限是根本性的。

00:01:30.692 --> 00:01:36.382
There are laws of nature we may never be able to understand, however advanced our brains might become.
无论我们的大脑变得多么发达，有些自然法则我们也许永远都无法理解。

00:01:37.182 --> 00:01:39.982
There are experiments we might never be able to perform.
有些实验我们也许永远都无法进行。

00:01:40.782 --> 00:01:47.172
We may never be able to test what caused the universe to be created, and what caused the cause of the universe being created.
我们也许永远无法检验是什么导致宇宙被创造出来，以及这一起因本身又是由什么引起的。

00:01:47.972 --> 00:01:50.162
This is where science may never break through.
这正是科学也许永远无法突破的地方。

00:01:50.962 --> 00:01:56.552
For something to be considered scientific, it must, by definition, be testable.
按照定义，某样东西要被视为科学的，就必须是可检验的。

00:01:57.352 --> 00:02:04.894
There is a problem here: it may not need to be testable right now, but it must be testable at some point in the future by experiment.
这里有个问题：它不必现在就能检验，但必须在将来的某个时候能够通过实验来检验。

00:02:05.694 --> 00:02:08.974
If an idea is untestable, that doesn’t mean it is wrong.
如果一个想法无法检验，那并不意味着它是错误的。

00:02:09.774 --> 00:02:11.754
It means it is untestable for now.
那只意味着它目前还无法检验。

00:02:12.554 --> 00:02:20.174
These untestable ideas also happen to be some of the most interesting ones, probably because they’ve puzzled humanity for centuries.
这些无法检验的想法也恰好属于最有趣的想法之列，这可能是因为它们已经困扰了人类好几个世纪。
